My Timemore Dot coffee scale has no screen, no SDK, and no protocol documentation. To read the weight, I decoded the BLE traffic myself.

## BLE for people who never touched it

A BLE device is a GATT server: it exposes a table of services, each holding characteristics, each optionally with descriptors. The phone is the client. You call `connectGatt()` to get a `BluetoothGatt`, `discoverServices()` to see the table, and then you read characteristics, write them, or subscribe to notifications.

Notifications are opt-in twice. You call `setCharacteristicNotification()` on the phone, and you write the Client Characteristic Configuration descriptor (`0x2902`) so the device knows to push. Data then shows up in `onCharacteristicChanged()`.

Bonding is `createBond()`: an OS-level pairing that stores a key and gives you link encryption, separate from GATT. MTU is the packet size, and the default is 23 bytes, which leaves 20 bytes of payload. If you expect more, you ask for it with `requestMtu()`. The [Android BLE guide](https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/bluetooth/ble/connect-gatt-server) covers the rest.

This scale exposes one service, `fff0`, with two characteristics: `fff1` notifies (scale to app) and `fff2` writes without response (app to scale).

## The handshake

The working order is:

<Mermaid diagram={`
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant Scale
    App->>Scale: connectGatt, retry up to 3x at 500 ms
    App->>Scale: requestMtu(247)
    App->>Scale: createBond
    App->>Scale: discoverServices
    App->>Scale: subscribe fff1 notifications
    Note over App: 500 ms
    App->>Scale: unit grams
    Note over App: 200 ms
    App->>Scale: standard mode, battery query
    Scale-->>App: weight notifications
`} />

The scale only streams over an encrypted link, so if you skip bonding you get silence. Bonding is also one-slot: the scale remembers a single bonded device and drops any other with an HCI `0x3E` error, so re-pairing means holding the button until the LED blinks fast. The first connect after a long idle often fails with status 133, so I retry instead of declaring it broken. I request an MTU of 247 because real notifications carry several frames: the largest capture was 36 bytes, and 15 of 3,419 idle notifications exceed the 20-byte default, so they would have been split.

## Framing: `A5 5A`

Every frame looks like this:

```plaintext
A5 5A <type> <cmd> <len_hi> <len_lo> <payload…> <crc_hi> <crc_lo>
```

`type` is `01` for notification, `02` for read, `03` for write/ack. The length field is two bytes, big-endian.

Outgoing frames carry a CRC-16/MODBUS checksum (init `0xFFFF`, reflected polynomial `0xA001`) over every byte before it, big-endian. That big-endian order already deviates from the MODBUS convention, which sends the CRC low byte first, and you only notice by computing the value. The "set unit to grams" frame `A5 5A 03 06 00 01 00` checksums to `E8 A7`, so the app sends `A5 5A 03 06 00 01 00 E8 A7`.

Incoming frames are the opposite. Every one of the 3,931 received frames in my two real captures ends in `00 00`: the scale never fills in the checksum. A valid incoming weight body would checksum to `0x82E6`, and it still arrives as `00 00`. If you validate the received CRC, you drop every valid reading. So the rule is: validate what you send, never what you receive.

The layout came from community notes, and I confirmed each field against the device.

## One notification is not one frame

A single notification can hold several frames (I saw an ack, a battery read, and a weight update packed into one 36-byte notification), and a frame can be split across two notifications. So the decoder's job is mostly resynchronization, not parsing.

The loop: append to a buffer, scan for `A5 5A`, read the declared length, drop anything above 64 as out of sync, wait for the whole frame, emit it, repeat. I keep a trailing `A5` because its `5A` might arrive in the next chunk. The buffer is capped at 512 bytes so a garbage stream cannot grow forever, and I reset it on reconnect.

## The payload

The `01 01` frame is the weight update, measured at 10.01 Hz on my unit. It is 9 bytes:

```kotlin
weightGrams = int32(p, 0) / 10.0         // int32 big-endian, ÷10 g
flowGramsPerSecond = int16(p, 4) / 10.0  // int16 big-endian, ÷10 g/s
timerSeconds = uint16(p, 6)              // uint16 seconds
overload = p.size > 8 && p[8] != 0       // overload flag in byte 8
```

A real frame `00 00 04 DA 02 24 00 00 00` decodes to 124.2 g at 54.8 g/s with the timer at 0.

Battery is read with `02 05`, and it comes back as two bytes `[bars, percent]` (`03 64` is 3 bars, 100 %). The scale also pushes that payload on its own, but not on a reliable schedule, so the app polls every 60 seconds and takes the push whenever it shows up. Commands are writes: `03 0D` tare, `03 02 [01|02|03]` timer start/pause/reset, `03 06 [00]` set grams, `03 08 [01 00]` standard mode, `02 13` read model. Each is acknowledged with `03 <cmd> 00 01 01`.

## Trusting the numbers

The raw flow saturates on a sudden load (99.9 g/s on my unit), so I clamp it to 0–25 g/s and smooth it. That clamp is a decision in the app, not a limit of the scale, which reports higher. There is no stability flag at all, so stability has to be computed in the app. That is where "just read the weight" turns into a data problem. The overload flag turned on around 1300 g on this unit, and an idle session stayed connected for 5 min 42 s with no keep-alive. Both of those are measurements on one device, not a datasheet.

I keep the recorded notifications as JSONL and replay them through the decoder in `RecordedSessionTest`, which asserts no unknown frames, the overload event, the unit and mode acknowledgements, and about 3,400 weight readings in the idle capture. What the app does with those readings after that is a whole other post.
